Revise go-io.md in place, holding every GO-IO ID stable. GO-IO-14 is a MUST with no exemplar precedent (go-io.md 'New commitments'). Its post-Wait kill(-pgid, SIGKILL) must be shown not to signal an unrelated process group.
Inputs:
- go-io.md: GO-IO-06/07/14 and conflicts 6-7;
- fixtures/go-io-consolidation/procgroup/;
- /home/mherwig/dev/ocx-sdk-python/src/ocx_sdk/_process.py, the pid-recycling guard near :803.
Read the specs: POSIX setpgid(2) and kill(2), plus the Linux man pages for pid reuse and process-group lifetime. Establish exactly when a PGID can be reused: once no process remains in the group?
Read the Go sources at go1.27.1:
- src/os/exec/exec.go: Cancel, WaitDelay, awaitGoroutines;
- src/syscall/exec_linux.go: Setpgid, Pdeathsig;
- src/os/pidfd_linux.go: how Process uses a pidfd since 1.23.
Search golang/go issues for Pdeathsig's thread-vs-process semantics and cite what is found. Do not assume it.
Plant three cases and record each exit status:
(a) a SIGTERM-ignoring grandchild alive after Wait; the sweep must kill it;
(b) an empty group; kill must return ESRCH;
(c) the SDK's own process SIGKILLed mid-run, with and without Pdeathsig, recording whether the children survive.
Reason from source about pgid reuse between Wait's reap and the sweep. If the window is real, design and test the guard: sweep only while a known member is unreaped, order Wait after the sweep, or another shape. Say which probes are themselves racy.
Record that macOS has no Pdeathsig, verified with GOOS=darwin go vet only.
Decide:
- GO-IO-14's exact text: sweep kept as is, guarded, or replaced;
- whether Pdeathsig becomes a SHOULD for the SDK on Linux;
- the per-OS table.
